    "The above table and graph show how average hourly wages difffer by both gender and marital status. Male workers generally earn higher wages than female workers and this pattern is consistent across both marital groups. In general, married individuals tend to earn more than non-married individuals, suggesting marital status is associated with wage differences. In the table, we can observe that education and employment follow similar trends. The average years of employment are marginally higher among married workers, and males are relatively high than females in the same marital group. Overall, the grouped summaries indicate that wage outcomes differ across demographic groups, with married males earning the highest wages and unmarried females the lowest."
