   "source": [
    "#Treating 'married' as an integer indicator\n",
    "df[\"married\"] = df[\"married\"].astype(int)\n",
    "\n",
    "#fit OLS model\n",
    "model = sm.OLS.from_formula(\n",
    "    'hourly_wage ~ years_in_education + years_in_employment + C(gender) + married', data=df).fit()\n",
    "model.summary()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "45ee36d5-88d0-45f6-9f61-23e21567b47b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Key points from the regression output:**\n",
    "\n",
    "* The R-squared values are approximately one-third, indicating that the model captures a third of the variation in hourly wages. This is not ideal; nonetheless, it suggests that there is an actual relationship between the predictors and wages.\n",
    "* Coefficients on years in education and years in employment are positive and statistically significant, implying that further schooling and work experience is linked to higher hourly wages, other factors being equal.\n",
    "* The male gender indicator is positive and significantly high. It means that male workers continue to earn more on average than female workers after adjusting education, experience, and marital status, which is compatible with the previous descriptive analysis.\n",
    "* The coefficient of marriage is also positive and significant, indicating that married people are more likely to earn higher wages than those who are not married and have similar characteristics.\n",
    "* Overall, the model justifies the observation that the human capital (education and experience) variables and demographic factors (gender, marital status) play a role in explaining wage disparities in this data set."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8c927a1c-b5be-4519-99f5-9148f67dae68",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## **Conclusion**\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d13bcf91-6866-438d-8b60-c9d50bb2bd4b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "This notebook has conducted an organized exploratory data analysis of a wage dataset, in the necessary chain of steps in the assessment brief:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Descriptive statistics showed that there was a wide range of differentiation in wages, education, and work experience, and also showed the sample composition in terms of gender and race.  \n",
    "2. Missing data were determined and treated by means of median and mode input, to use as much data as possible.  \n",
    "3. A set of visualisations (histograms, scatter plots, boxplots, grouped bar charts) gave an understanding of the distributions of the variables of interest and their correlations with each other.  \n",
    "4. Frequency tables and a contingency analysis helped to explain the distribution and association of categorical variables to each other.  \n",
    "5. Subsetting and grouped summaries were used to demonstrate that data on combinations of characteristics (education, experience, gender, marital status) are related to varying wage results.  \n",
    "6. The two sample t-test (Hypothesis testing) formally indicated a statistically significant gender wage gap in the sample.  \n",
    "7. The effect of education, experience, gender, and marital status on hourly wages was estimated using a multiple linear regression model, which indicated that the four variables can account for a significant proportion of the wage variation together.\n"
   ]
  },
